  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\text{_____}\\,k\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z}=0.$\n What is the third component of $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1-{e}^{z} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{z} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1 \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{4}+1)\\,k.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1)\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({u}^{2}+{v}^{4}+1).$",
